/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Let σmax and σmin be the largest and smallest stresses in the cycle, in MPa, with tension positive.
   - σm = (σmax + σmin)/2 σa = (σmax − σmin)/2 R = σmin/σmax
   - σm is the mean stress, σa the alternating stress (half the range), both in MPa, and R the stress ratio.
   - Three cycles have names.
   - Plot σm along the horizontal axis and σa up the vertical one (the Haigh diagram).
   - Each fatigue line starts at Se on the σa axis and ends at a static strength on the σm axis; none is drawn past that intercept.
   - Soderberg is the lowest line because Syt < Sut, so it is the safest and also guards against yield.
   - Scale the working point by n until it reaches the chosen line:
   - Goodman: 1/n = σa/Se + σm/Sut Soderberg: 1/n = σa/Se + σm/Syt Gerber: (σm/Sut)² n² + (σa/Se) n − 1 = 0
   - n is the factor of safety; for Gerber take the positive root.
*/
