/* book exam traps: Taking the impact factor as 1 for a suddenly applied load. It is 2. | Writing the work as Wh only. The weight keeps falling while the rod stretches, so the work is W(h + δ). | Mixing units in 2h/δst: h in metres and δst in millimetres throws the root off by a factor of about 30. | Thinking a stronger, stiffer rod always helps. A smaller δst raises the impact factor; extra flexibility often helps more than extra strength. */
